<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stephen Decatur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1779">1779</a>–<a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephen Decatur was an officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a>, famous for burning the captured frigate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in Tripoli harbour in 1804 during the First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> War. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> he captured the British frigate Macedonian. In 1815 he led a squadron to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> in the Second Barbary War and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> to end tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and free American captives. He was killed in a duel in 1820.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stephen-decatur.jpg" alt="Stephen Decatur" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Bird King · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Bird_King_-_Stephen_Decatur_-_NPG.87.26_-_National_Portrait_Gallery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Decatur was born on 5 January 1779 at Sinepuxent, in Worcester County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, where his parents had fled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> during the British occupation. His father, Stephen Decatur Sr., was a merchant captain who served as an officer in the navy during the Revolution, and his mother was Ann Pine. The family returned to Philadelphia, where Decatur graduated from the Episcopal Academy. In 1797 he worked for the shipbuilders Gurney and Smith on the frigate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and in 1798 he began his naval career as a midshipman aboard that ship under John Barry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the First Barbary War Decatur served from 13 May 1801 as first lieutenant of the frigate Essex. For the raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli-greece">Tripoli</a> harbour he sailed in the Intrepid with 80 volunteers, and his promotion to captain, dated 16 February 1804, made him at twenty-five the youngest man to hold that rank in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a>. On 8 March 1806 he married Susan Wheeler, daughter of the mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. He supervised the building of gunboats, sat on the court martial that found Commodore James Barron guilty of unpreparedness after the Chesapeake–Leopard affair, and in 1809 patrolled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> coast in the Chesapeake to enforce the Embargo Act.</p><p>In the War of 1812 Decatur's squadron was trapped at New London by a British squadron under Sir Thomas Masterman Hardy from June 1813 until the end of the war. On 20 May 1815 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> gave him command of the frigate Guerriere and a squadron of ten ships for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As a member of the Board of Navy Commissioners Decatur settled in Washington, where in 1818 he built a house on Lafayette Square designed by Benjamin Henry Latrobe, the first private residence near the White House. He strongly opposed the reinstatement of Barron, who had returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, and Barron challenged him to a duel, in which Decatur was killed on 22 March 1820. Congress had awarded him a gold medal on 29 January 1813 for the capture of the Macedonian. Five ships of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> have been named Decatur, and his house is now a museum of the National Trust for Historic Preservation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stephen Decatur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1815 an American squadron under <b>Stephen Decatur</b> forced a treaty ending American tribute payments, and in August 1816 an Anglo-Dutch fleet under Lord Exmouth bombarded Algiers and obtained the release of more than a thousand Christian captives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Much of its idea and some of its wording came from his earlier song &quot;When the Warrior Returns&quot;, written for <b>Stephen Decatur</b> and Charles Stewart after the First Barbary War and set to the same tune.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-star-spangled-banner">The Star-Spangled Banner</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Berlin, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Bladensburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.33,-75.22&amp;year=1805" aria-label="Berlin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Stephen Decatur"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5981 4226h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5981 4226h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.33,-75.22&amp;year=1805">What was here in 1805? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
